From 47c70835a65c800410c9dd2fac62fa4c1f6ddcb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(dsa-indexer): break exact top-k score ties by lowest
 position

The radix top-k handed the slots left at its threshold to whichever tied
candidates reached a shared-memory counter first: the buffered arm's last
round decremented a remaining-slot counter, and the exact overflow
fallback's final pass incremented the output counter. Candidates whose
float32 scores are bitwise equal were therefore chosen by thread timing.
With MXFP4 index queries and keys such ties are common: on DeepSeek V4.1
Flash two identical runs selected different sets for 78% of layer 2's
prefill rows, about 5 of 512 positions each. DeepSeek's reference selects
with torch.topk, which repeats for the same scores, and the technical report
sets no tie rule.

Both paths now collect the tied candidates and keep the ones with the lowest
logical positions. The buffered arm gathers its last-round ties in the free
candidate buffer; the fallback filters the row by the pivot score. An exact
4-round 8-bit radix over the descending position key then finds the cut.
Positions are unique within a row, including carried candidates, which keep
their logical index, so the chosen set is a function of the scores alone and
agrees across chunked folds. The compile-cache policies are bumped so cached
cubins of the old behavior never load. The FP8 fused indexer keeps its own
selection unchanged.
